        "text": "may have different access levels to objects.\nIn contrast to DAC or MAC, where subjects have access to objects based strictly on the\npermissions involved, the RBAC model represents a logical abstraction over MAC or DAC,\nwhere the subjects must be members of a specific group or role before interacting with objects.\n\u2022 Multi-level security ( MLS ) is a specific MAC scheme where the subjects are processes and the\nobjects are files, sockets, and other similar system resources.\n\u2022 Multi-category security ( MCS ) is an improved version of SELinux that allows users to label\nfiles with categories . MCS reuses much of the MLS framework in SELinux.\nIntroducing SELinux\n325\nWrapping up our brief discussion on ACMs, we should note that we covered some of the internals of DAC\nand ACL in Chapter 4 , Managing Users and Groups , particularly in the Managing permissions section.\nNext, we\u2019ll turn our attention to SELinux\u2014a first-class citizen for MAC implementations.",
